          "deepDive": "**AといおうかBといおうか** (literally *shall I say A, shall I say B*) is the speaker thinking aloud while hunting for the right word. The おう is the volitional of 言う (いおう), so the feel is *should I call it A… or B…?* The two labels are offered as approximations; the speaker commits to neither, and the sentence usually ends with a summary (とにかく〜, 全く言葉もない) or trails off (……).\n\nTwo typical uses:\n- **Searching for a description**: 砂漠のオアシスといおうか、嵐の夜の灯台といおうか — piling up images to capture something hard to name.\n- **Softening criticism**: 積極的すぎるといおうか、言い方がストレートといおうか… Naming the fault indirectly and vaguely makes it less harsh. In the sample, the detective uses it with sarcasm: 間が抜けているといおうか、無責任といおうか — both options are insults, and the hedge only makes it more cutting.\n\nForms and variants:\n- Attaches to plain forms; な-adjectives and nouns drop だ: 無責任といおうか, 個性的といおうか.\n- **といおうか** can be used once (庶民感覚を知らないといおうか……).\n- **というか…というか** is the everyday colloquial version (the most common in speech); **というべきか…というべきか** is more written and deliberate (運がよかったというべきか悪かったというべきか).\n- **〜といおうか何といおうか** = *or whatever you'd call it*; a fixed way to show you're lost for words.\n\nCompare:\n- **N₁といいN₂といい** (#44): *both N₁ and N₂ (are examples of the same evaluation)* — picks out concrete examples to support a judgment; といおうか instead offers alternative *names* for one thing.\n- **〜やら〜やら / 〜だの〜だの**: list several things; no sense of searching for the right word.\n\nJLPT tip: the part after といおうか…といおうか should sum up or match the two labels. If the labels are negative (分析が甘い, 論旨があいまい), the conclusion is a negative evaluation or a suggestion to fix it.",
