          "deepDive": "**〜のをいいことに** literally means *treating ~ as a good thing*: someone sees a situation (nobody watching, the owner away, a delayed train) as a convenient opportunity and **exploits it to do something they shouldn't**. The speaker always disapproves — it is a critical expression, never neutral.\n\nStructure: [situation]のをいいことに、[someone's selfish or bad behavior]. The situation is usually something that removes a restraint: 誰も見ていない, 親が叱らない, 疑われていない, 留守, 社長の甥である.\n\nForms:\n- Plain form + の + をいいことに: 見ていないのをいいことに, 遅れたのをいいことに.\n- な-adj/noun: 静かなのをいいことに / 甥であるのをいいことに.\n- **N + をいいことに** directly with nouns: 留守をいいことに, 匿名をいいことに, それをいいことに.\n\nPitfalls:\n- The second half is the behavior of the person taking advantage, and it must be something bad or selfish. ✗天気がいいのをいいことに、公園を散歩した sounds wrong (walking isn't reprehensible) — use 天気がいいので.\n- Don't confuse with **〜をきっかけに** (*triggered by ~*, neutral/positive) or **〜を機に** (*taking the opportunity of ~*, positive).\n\nCompare **〜にかこつけて** (#64): *using ~ as a pretext* — the reason is an excuse the person gives, often to disguise their real purpose (仕事にかこつけて旅行する). のをいいことに is about exploiting a situation where no one can stop you.\n\nJLPT tip: if the blank sits between an 'opportunity' (nobody sees, nobody scolds) and a critical description of misbehavior, のをいいことに is the answer.",
