          "deepDive": "**N₁といわずN₂といわず** literally means *without saying N₁, without saying N₂*: whether you call it N₁ or N₂, it makes no difference — **everywhere / always / all of it**. The two nouns are representative examples of a whole range, and the sentence says the situation covers the whole range: 早朝といわず、深夜といわず = *at all hours, early morning and late at night alike*.\n\nTypical pairs: 昼といわず夜といわず (time), 床といわずベッドの上といわず (place), 顔といわず手といわず (body parts), 服といわず文具といわず (things), 先輩といわず後輩といわず (people).\n\nNuance:\n- It's written-style and somewhat emphatic, and often describes a situation that is **excessive, annoying or striking** (電話が鳴りっぱなし, 散乱している, 揺れて困る), though it can also be neutral (全部キティちゃんグッズで統一している).\n- The two nouns are usually a matched pair of the same category.\n- The second half describes a fact or state, not a request or a volition.\n\nCompare:\n- **N₁といいN₂といい** (#44): picks out N₁ and N₂ as examples to back up an **evaluation** of one thing (味といい値段といい、満足だ). といわず…といわず says the situation **extends everywhere**.\n- **〜を問わず** (N2): *regardless of ~*, neutral, used with a scale or category word (昼夜を問わず, 年齢を問わず). 昼夜を問わず is a close paraphrase of 昼といわず夜といわず.\n- **〜も〜も**: plain *both ~ and ~*.\n\nJLPT tip: in the Check, MP3といわずフィギュアといわず、全部買っている — a list of examples + 全部/いつも/どこでも in the second half signals といわず…といわず.",
