          "xref": "☞ 69. 感謝の{念|ねん}**にたえません**",
          "deepDive": "**V-る / N + にたえない** uses 堪える (たえる, *to bear, to be worth*): 聞くにたえない = *not bearable to listen to* / *not worth listening to*. Something is **so bad, ugly, vulgar or painful** that one can't stand to perceive it. It is a strong, critical, written-style expression.\n\nRestricted verbs: it combines with a small set of perception verbs and nouns — 聞く, 見る, 読む, and the Sino-Japanese nouns 正視 (*looking straight at*) and 傾聴 (*listening attentively*): 聞くにたえない, 見るにたえない, 読むにたえない, 正視にたえない. Don't create new combinations like ✗食べるにたえない.\n\nNuance: it describes the object (an ugly scene, vulgar talk, a poorly written novel) from the speaker's disgusted or pained perspective. It can express both *disgust* (低俗な小説) and *pity/pain* (a tragic sight too sad to look at).\n\nThe ☞ points to a different use (#69): **N + にたえない / にたえません** after emotion nouns — 感謝の念にたえません, 喜びにたえない, 遺憾にたえない — meaning *I can't contain my ~ / I feel deep ~*. That one is a very formal expression of strong emotion, used in speeches and letters, with no negative evaluation of anything. Tell them apart by what comes before: perception verb (見る・聞く・読む) → *unbearable*; emotion noun (感謝・喜び・遺憾) → *overwhelmed with feeling*.\n\nAlso compare **〜に（は）たえる** (*to be worth ~*): 鑑賞にたえる作品 *a work worth appreciating*.\n\nJLPT tip: 騒音で聞くにたえない (Check) — a noise so bad you can't bear to listen.",
