          "deepDive": "**AならいざしらずB**: いざ知らず is an old expression meaning *I don't know (about that)*. So the pattern says *if it were A, I couldn't say (it might be acceptable / understandable), but B is different*. A is a special or extreme case in which the thing might be OK; B is the actual case, and the speaker judges it unacceptable or unreasonable.\n\nTypical A's: a different time (学生時代, 20年前, 昔), a different kind of person (天才, 一般の人間, プロ, 子ども), or a different condition (自分が不愉快な思いをした). The second half usually contains criticism, a firm judgment or a rhetorical question: 無理ですよ, 〜べきじゃないのか, おかしい, 〜わけがない.\n\nConnection: plain forms; な-adjectives and nouns attach without だ (上手ならいざしらず, 学生時代ならいざしらず). Also **〜はいざしらず** directly after a noun: 他の人はいざしらず、私は反対だ.\n\nCompare **〜ならまだしも** (#50): very close in meaning and structure. Differences:\n- まだしも concedes that A would be *more tolerable*; いざしらず sets A aside as *a case about which I can't say / a special case*.\n- ならいざしらず is more formal and literary (the formal mark). In the sample, the president uses it: 一般の人間ならいざしらず、警察の取り調べにも正体がバレなかったのなら… — *ordinary people might not notice, but even the police didn't*.\n\nAlso compare **〜はともかく** (*leaving A aside*) — more neutral.\n\nJLPT tip: 天才ならいざしらず、普通の人間が努力もせずに受かるわけがない (Check) — the special case (a genius) is dismissed, and the real case (an ordinary person) is judged.",
